Status: normative contract for `runtime/control_socket.*` and the status
replies of `yume` and `yumed`. `yume --status`, `yume --accept-route` and
`yumed --status` are its clients today, and the desktop GUI will be one. It is
a local interface between processes of one user, not a network protocol.

## Purpose

A running `yume` or `yumed` answers questions about its state and takes a few
requests on a UNIX socket. The GUI is a separate, unprivileged program that
talks to the running `yume` only through this socket, so it can crash or be
replaced without touching the tunnel. The rules below let newer programs add
fields and requests without breaking an older GUI, and let a newer GUI see
what an older program lacks.

## The socket

The configuration's `control.socket` names the socket's path, and
`yume --control-socket` names another for one run, so a program that starts
`yume` chooses where to find it. The directory that holds it must belong to
the running user and be closed to writes by group and others. The socket is mode 0600, and a connection from another user
is closed before it is read. The server serves at most 4 connections at once,
and later ones wait. Each ends after one reply, or after 2 seconds without a
complete request.

A connection carries one request and one reply:

1. The client sends one JSON object and a newline, at most 512 bytes.
2. The server sends one JSON object and a newline, under 64 KiB, and closes
   the connection.

Replies are UTF-8. Text from a peer or the system that is not valid UTF-8 is
replaced, so a reply is always one JSON line.

## Versions and extension

Every request and reply carries `"control": 1`. The number changes only for a
change an older client cannot survive, which needs a new version of this
document. Within protocol 1:

- Fields and requests are only added. An existing field keeps its name, type
  and meaning, and an existing request keeps its fields and its reply.
- A request is closed: it holds exactly the fields its definition lists.
- A reply is open: a client ignores fields it does not know and does not rely
  on their order.
- Every status reply lists, in `requests`, the requests that program takes. A
  client sends only a listed request. Any other gets `unknown_request`.
- A field described as present *when* something holds is absent otherwise. A
  field that can be `null` is always present.

A new request, such as a command the GUI needs later, gets a name, a closed
list of fields and a reply that names its result in a field of its own. A
request that changes anything says so in its definition here. No request
keeps its connection open: a client that follows a state asks for status
again.

## Errors

A request that fails gets an error reply:

@code text
{"control": 1, "error": "no route proposal has that id", "code": "not_found"}
@end

A client acts on `code`. The text is for people and may change.

@table
| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `malformed` | The request is not a JSON object, is too long, or lacks or adds a field |
| `unsupported` | `control` is missing or not 1 |
| `unknown_request` | This program does not take that request |
| `not_found` | The request names something that does not exist, such as a proposal id |
| `failed` | The program could not carry out a well-formed request |
| `unavailable` | The program could not produce its status |
@end

## Requests

`status`, taken by both programs, changes nothing:

@code text
{"control": 1, "request": "status"}
@end

`accept-route`, taken by a `yume` whose configuration has a `circuits`
section, accepts the shorter route a status reply proposes, by its `id`, a
string of at most 64 characters. It changes which routes the client uses. A
proposal is described in [cluster 1](CLUSTER_1.md):

@code text
{"control": 1, "request": "accept-route", "id": "00112233aabbccdd"}
{"control": 1, "accepted": "00112233aabbccdd"}
@end

An id that no current proposal has gets `not_found`, so an answer to a
proposal the user has not seen accepts nothing.

`messages`, taken by both programs, changes nothing. It returns the lines
the program has printed, numbered from 1 in order, after the number `after`:

@code text
{"control": 1, "request": "messages", "after": 0}
{"control": 1, "instance": "5f0c9a3e11d2b746", "messages": [{"seq": 1, "time": "2026-09-30T12:00:00.250Z", "text": "session authenticated"}], "missed": 0, "more": false}
@end

A program keeps its latest 256 lines, each cut to 512 bytes. The reply holds
the kept lines numbered above `after`, oldest first, each with its `seq`, its
`time` in UTC and its `text`, as many as fit in 63 KiB. `missed` counts the
lines numbered above `after` that the program no longer keeps, and `more` is
`true` when kept lines were left out, so the client asks again after the last
`seq` it received. `instance` is the same for the life of the program and
changes when it starts again, when numbering restarts at 1. The lines are the
ones the program writes to its standard error, and they hold no key,
credential or payload.

`stop`, taken by `yume`, changes the program's state: it answers, then stops
the program as `SIGTERM` does, closing its sessions and listeners and
removing the TUN networking it owns. A program that started `yume` stops it
this way:

@code text
{"control": 1, "request": "stop"}
{"control": 1, "stopping": true}
@end

The program stops also when the client leaves before reading the answer,
since the request itself was valid.

## The status of yume

@table
| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `control`, `requests` | 1, and the requests this program takes |
| `program`, `version` | `"yume"`, and the product version |
| `state` | `idle`, `connecting`, `connected`, `waiting` between attempts, or `closed` |
| `server` | `host` and `port` of the server the kit names |
| `server_identity` | When connected: the server's verified composite fingerprint |
| `connected_ms` | When connected: how long the session has been up |
| `retry_ms` | When waiting: the delay before the next attempt |
| `sessions` | Sessions authenticated since the program started |
| `failed_attempts` | Failed attempts since the last authenticated session |
| `last_failure` | `null`, or the latest failure's `code` and `message` |
| `traffic` | `payload_bytes_sent`, `payload_bytes_received`, `record_bytes_sent` and `record_bytes_received`, summed over every session |
| `socks5` | The SOCKS5 listeners, as `address:port` |
| `forwards` | The forward listeners, as `address:port` or a socket path |
| `posture` | The fixed composition and the tuning limits in use, below |
| `circuits` | `null`, or the circuits below when the configuration has a `circuits` section |
@end

`posture` holds what this build and configuration run, for a display that
states the posture rather than offering switches: the `transport` version
(`YTP/1`), its `suite`, the captured `evidence_profile` the outer layers
follow, the `security` provider and the `crypto_backend` library, as
`yume --version` prints them, and `limits`, the configuration's
`max_queued_bytes`, `max_epoch_bytes`, `credit_returns_per_window` and
`idle_epoch_rotation`, which the tuning presets set. When connected it also
holds `epoch_bytes`, the session's key epoch: the smaller of this client's
and the server's `max_epoch_bytes`.

`circuits` holds `hops`, `min_hops`, `accepted_hops` (0 unless a shorter route
was accepted), `current_hops` (the length new streams take, 0 while none can
be used), `plain_http` (`refuse` or `allow`), `serial` (the routes view's, 0
before one is verified), `stopped` (`null`, or why circuits stopped), `routes`
and `proposal`. Each route has its `nodes` by name, the entry first, its
`age_ms` and its open `streams`. `proposal` is `null`, or a shorter route's
`id`, `hops`, `nodes`, the view's `serial`, what the user `gives_up` in plain
words, and `latency_ms` when every hop has been measured.

## The status of yumed

@table
| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `control`, `requests` | 1, and the requests this program takes |
| `program`, `version` | `"yumed"`, and the product version |
| `listeners` | The listening endpoints, as `address:port` |
| `client_sessions` | Client sessions open now |
| `cluster` | `null`, or the cluster below when the configuration names one |
@end

`cluster` holds the list's `id` (the operator's fingerprint), `serial`,
`not_after` in UTC, whether it has `expired`, this server's name as `self`,
its `links` and its `circuits`. Each link names the `peer` and its `identity`,
the `outbound` link this server keeps (its `state`, `sessions`,
`failed_attempts`, `last_failure`, and `connected_ms` or `retry_ms` as for
`yume`), the `inbound` sessions the peer holds here (`sessions`, and
`connected_ms` when there is one) and the `circuits` it carries `in` and
`out`. `circuits` counts whether this server is an `exit`, circuits `open`,
those it is the `entry` of, those it `relayed`, `exit_streams`, `failed`
circuits and those `refused`, in total and by bound (`client_circuits`,
`circuit_rate`, `handshakes`, `streams`). No count names both neighbours of a
circuit.

## Limits

- Replies hold no key, credential, admission secret or payload. They do name
  server identities, cluster node names, local listener addresses and the
  program's printed lines, which the socket's permissions keep to the user
  who runs the program.
- Any process of that user can send `accept-route` and `stop`. That user could
  edit the configuration or end the process anyway.
- The socket is not a stable interface for other users or other hosts, and
  the C ABI does not serve it.
